## Execution Phases

### Phase 0: Data Restructuring (data.js)

**Priority: Do this first. Everything else depends on it.**

**Objective:** Restructure all info cards into the three-type taxonomy, and restructure all consequences into the stepped moments format. Keep old fields alongside new ones during migration so the existing UI doesn't break.

**The three card types:**

**ENTITY** — For content primarily about a specific company or competitor. Has front/back data (front = overview stats, back = intel briefing). Used for: Apple, Nokia, BlackBerry, Google, HTC, Samsung, Motorola, etc.

**MEMO** — For internal Microsoft communications. Has FROM/RE header, classification badge, structured sections. Used for: Pieter Knook's strategic position memo, Andy Lees's WM7 delay, Robbie Bach's Project Pink, WP7 launch assessment, Surface post-mortem, board pressure reports.

**BRIEFING** — For market data and thematic analysis. Has hero stat at top, structured data sections. Used for: iPhone launch analysis, BYOD trends, app gap analysis, developer sentiment, market share reports, financial analyses.

**Classification logic for each existing card:**
- Does the card have a FROM: line or read like an internal Microsoft document? → MEMO
- Is the card primarily about a single named company's position/strategy? → ENTITY
- Is the card about market trends, data, projections, or ecosystem analysis? → BRIEFING
- Some cards bundle multiple companies (e.g., "Nokia & RIM Response" in D1, "OEM Partner Defections" in D2b). These should either be split into individual entity cards OR kept as a single BRIEFING with a "Competitor Landscape" framing — use judgment based on how many cards the decision already has. Cap at 5-6 cards per decision.

**For each decision point, restructure info cards from the current format:**

```javascript
// CURRENT
"d1-info-market": {
    id: "d1-info-market",
    type: "Market Report",
    title: "iPhone Launch Analysis",
    visual: '<i class="ph ph-chart-line" ...></i>',
    summary: "...",
    content: `<p>Long HTML blob...</p>`,
    source: "...",
    date: "...",
    quality: "high"
}
```

**Into the new format (see d1-data-template.js for complete example):**

```javascript
// NEW — BRIEFING type
{
    id: "d1-intel-iphone-market",
    type: "briefing",
    thumb: {
        icon: "📊",
        label: "MARKET REPORT",
        title: "iPhone Launch Analysis",
        subtitle: "$499 · AT&T · No apps",
        accent: "#7ab88e"
    },
    popup: {
        title: "...",
        source: "...",
        date: "...",
        reliability: "HIGH",
        heroStat: { value: "1%", label: "...", attribution: "..." },
        sections: [
            { heading: "...", content: "..." },
            { heading: "...", type: "stats", items: [...] },
            { heading: "...", type: "list", items: [...] },
        ],
        footnote: "..."
    }
}
```

**For consequences, restructure from:**

```javascript
// CURRENT
consequence: {
    immediate: "<p>Wall Street approves...</p><p>The iPhone launches...</p>",
    delayed: "<p>Android announced...</p>",
    metrics: { cash: +1000, marketShare: -5 }
}
```

**Into:**

```javascript
// NEW
consequence: {
    moments: [
        { type: "verdict", headline: "...", subline: "...", sentiment: "positive" },
        { type: "metrics", changes: [
            { metric: "Market Cap", change: "+$1B", direction: "up" },
            ...
        ]},
        { type: "timeline", events: [
            { date: "JUN 29", text: "...", mood: "neutral" },
            ...
        ]},
        { type: "emerging", icon: "⚡", headline: "...", body: "...", closing: "..." }
    ],
    metricDeltas: {
        immediate: { cash: 0, marketCap: 1000, ... },
        delayed: { marketShare: -15, ... }
    },
    pathState: { d1Choice: "wait", d2Branch: "d2a" }
}
```

**Important:** Keep the existing `metricDeltas` and `pathState` structures unchanged — these feed into state.js which must not break. The `moments` array is purely for UI rendering.

**Approach:**
1. Start with D1 using the template as the exact reference. Get D1 perfect.
2. Then systematically convert D2a, D2b, D3, D4, D5.
3. For each, read the existing HTML content carefully and extract into the structured format. Don't lose information — restructure it.
4. Each decision's info cards will have different numbers and types. The card taxonomy per decision should be roughly:

| Decision | Cards | Likely Types |
|----------|-------|-------------|
| D1 | 5 | 1 briefing, 1 memo, 3 entities |
| D2a | 3 | 1 briefing (BYOD), 1 memo (WM7), 1 entity (Google) |
| D2b | 3-5 | 1 memo (Pink), 1 briefing (Android), 1 briefing or 3 entities (OEMs) |
| D3 | 5-6 | 2 briefings (market, BYOD/dev), 1 memo (WP7 launch), 2-3 entities (Nokia, iPad) |
| D4 | 5 | 2 briefings (app gap, market), 1 entity (Nokia financial), 2 memos (Surface, board) |
| D5 | TBD | Follow pattern |

5. For the consequence moments, not every consequence needs all 4 moment types. The "verdict" and "metrics" moments are universal. "timeline" works when there's a multi-month progression to show. "emerging" works as a bridge to the next decision. Use judgment.


### Phase 1: Card Popup System (ui.js)

**Objective:** Build three popup components and a thumbnail grid system.

**What to build:**

1. `renderCardPopup(cardData)` — A router function that checks `cardData.type` and dispatches to the correct popup renderer.

2. `renderEntityPopup(cardData)` — Modal overlay with front/back flip animation. Front: icon, name, quickStats grid, sentiment badge, "Flip for intel" button. Back: key signal quote, detail rows, analysis note, "Flip back" button.

3. `renderMemoPopup(cardData)` — Modal overlay, single-sided. FROM/RE header block, classification stamp (e.g., "INTERNAL"), structured sections that support prose, highlighted prose (with left border accent), and bullet lists.

4. `renderBriefingPopup(cardData)` — Modal overlay, single-sided. Hero stat displayed large at top. Title. Structured sections supporting prose, stats grids, and lists. Footnote at bottom.

5. `renderCardThumbnail(cardData, onClick)` — Type-aware compact card for the grid. Each type has a visually distinct header strip and label color:
   - Entity: gradient header, coral "COMPETITOR" label, sentiment tag
   - Memo: muted warm header, gold "INTERNAL MEMO" label, FROM name
   - Briefing: muted green header, green "MARKET REPORT" label, key number

6. `renderIntelGrid(cards)` — Renders the 2x2 (or wider) grid of thumbnails. Layout logic:
   - 4 or fewer cards: 2x2 grid, equal size
   - 5 cards: 2x2 for primary 4 + 1 full-width row for secondary card below
   - 6+ cards: 2x2 for top 4 + "More Intel" expand button revealing the rest

**Shared popup infrastructure:**
- All three popups share the same overlay (fixed position, blurred backdrop, click-outside-to-close)
- All have a source/reliability footer bar
- All have a close (×) button
- All should disable body scroll when open and restore scroll position on close

**Visual reference:** See the prototype (mobile-wars-v3.jsx) for exact rendering of all three types. Match the color system, typography, and spacing.


### Phase 2: Unified Briefing + Choice Page (ui.js)

**Objective:** Merge the info briefing and decision choice into a single scrollable page.

**Currently:** `renderDecisionPoint()` shows objective + info cards + options all inline. The transition between briefing info and choice happens within this function.

**New behavior:** `renderDecisionPoint()` should render a single continuous page:

```
[Objective: "How should Microsoft respond..."]
[Intel Briefing label]
[Card thumbnail grid — 2x2 or wider]
["Tap any card for full briefing" hint]
[Your Position — Microsoft card]
— divider —
[Your Call label]
[Option 1 — collapsed: title + investment]
[Option 2 — collapsed: title + investment]
[Option 3 — collapsed: title + investment]
[Option 4 — collapsed: title + investment]
[Confirm button — appears after selection]
```

**Key interaction:** Options are accordion-style. Only title + investment visible when collapsed. Tap to expand — reveals description, risk, upside, and "Choose This Path" CTA. Only one option expanded at a time. After selection, unselected options dim to 30% opacity, confirm button appears.

**Why this matters:** User can scroll up to re-check intel cards, tap one for detail, close popup, scroll back down to options. Zero friction between information gathering and decision making.


### Phase 3: Consequence Stepper (ui.js + state.js)

**Objective:** Replace the single-screen consequence dump with a stepped moment-by-moment reveal.

**state.js changes:**

Add consequence step tracking:

```javascript
// Add to state
consequenceStep: 0,

// Add methods
advanceConsequenceStep() {
    this.consequenceStep++;
},

resetConsequenceStep() {
    this.consequenceStep = 0;
}
```

Call `resetConsequenceStep()` when entering the consequence phase. Call `advanceConsequenceStep()` on each "Next" click. Only call `continueToNext()` on the final step.

**ui.js changes:**

Rewrite `renderConsequence()` to read `gameState.consequenceStep` and render the corresponding moment from the `moments` array.

Each moment type has a distinct renderer:

- **verdict** — Large headline text, subline in italic, sentiment indicator (📈/📉/⚖️). Single screen, centered.
- **metrics** — 2x2 grid of metric change cards with animated numbers. Color-coded by direction (green up, red down, gold neutral).
- **timeline** — Vertical timeline with date markers, event descriptions, and mood-colored dots (green = positive, gold = warning, neutral = dim). Events stagger in with slight delay.
- **emerging** — Icon, bold headline in red, body text, closing line in italic with border-top separator. This is the dramatic bridge to the next decision.

**Progress indicator:** Horizontal dots at the top showing current step and total steps. Active dot is wider (24px vs 6px), coral colored.

**Navigation:** "Next →" button (outline style) for steps 1-3. "Continue →" button (filled coral) for the final step, which triggers `continueToNext()`.

**Choice recap:** Small banner at the top of the consequence phase showing "You chose: [option name]" with a coral left border.

**DO NOT CHANGE:** The branching logic, pathState system, or how `confirmDecision()` and `continueToNext()` chain stages. The stepper is purely a rendering change — the same game logic fires, just at the end of the stepped sequence instead of immediately.


### Phase 4: Time Passage & Chapter Intros (ui.js + transitions.js)

**Objective:** Add a richer time passage screen between decisions and a chapter intro for each new decision.

**Time passage screen:**

After the final consequence moment and before the next decision's story point, show a time passage interstitial:

```
[Date label — e.g., "SEP 2009"]
[Spinning ring animation]
["32 months pass..."]
[Metric shift cards — animated in sequence]
  Windows Mobile Share: 42% → 20% (red)
  Market Clarity: Uncertain → iPhone + Android win (gold)
  Strategic Options: Many → Fewer (red)
[Next Decision → button]
```

The metric shifts should animate in with staggered delays (not all at once). Each card slides in from the left with a colored left border matching its sentiment.

This could be implemented as:
- A new function `renderTimePassage(fromMetrics, toMetrics, onComplete)` in ui.js
- OR an extension of the existing `Transitions.showPreDecisionTransition()` in transitions.js
- Recommend: new function in ui.js. Cleaner separation from the 3-second interstitial which serves a different purpose.

**Chapter intro screen:**

When transitioning to a new decision, show a brief chapter intro before the narrative:

```
[Circle with decision number]
[Decision label — "Decision Two"]
[Title — "The Android Threat"]
[1-2 line teaser text connecting to previous choice]
```

This data should live in data.js as part of each decision's config:

```javascript
chapter: {
    number: 2,
    label: "Decision Two",
    title: "The Android Threat",
    teaser: "Android is exploding. Your 'wait and watch' strategy gave you perfect clarity — on exactly how far behind you've fallen."
}
```

The teaser text can be path-sensitive (using `framingByPath` logic that already exists in the codebase).

